WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.880
Speaker 1: Today we're going to&nbsp;
be speaking with Chris Finan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:06.840
who's the executive director of the&nbsp;
National Coalition Against Censorship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:12.660
and also author of the book How Free Speech&nbsp;
Saved Democracy. Chris, really great having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:14.640
you on. I appreciate it.
Speaker 5: Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:18.300
thanks. I appreciate you having me.
Speaker 1: You know, we've had conversations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:26.160
about the First Amendment that were very legally&nbsp;
oriented and we've had those often with law&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:32.580
professors and others. One of the interesting&nbsp;
things about your book is that you delve more into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:40.680
the history of when the suppression of speech and&nbsp;
the fight for free speech were. You contextualize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:45.180
it in history in some interesting ways, and one&nbsp;
of the things you point to just to start somewhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:51.660
and we could start anywhere. You talk about war as&nbsp;
being one of the big culprits used by governments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:56.280
to suppress free speech. And I think that won't&nbsp;
come as a huge shock to many in our audience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:03.240
when we understand the idea of for the purposes&nbsp;
of supporting the troops for national security,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:08.760
whatever, don't say this or say that. That, I&nbsp;
think will make sense. But can you talk about some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:15.960
of the examples of when war was used in this way?
Speaker 5: Sure. Because actually war. World War&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:24.360
One was a civil liberties meltdown in this&nbsp;
country and actually precipitated lawyers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:30.300
for the first time to start thinking about using&nbsp;
the First Amendment to protect speech that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:41.640
government might not agree with. You know, the&nbsp;
the the Sedition Act and passed by Congress in at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:50.160
the behest of President Wilson actually provided&nbsp;
prison sentences of up to ten years for people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:59.040
spoke out against the war. And it was ultimately&nbsp;
used to prosecute over 2000 Americans, some for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:09.000
the most inane kinds of criticisms like Wilson&nbsp;
as a block or people who circulated petitions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:18.240
you know the most today. What we would say is&nbsp;
the most frequent forms of speech were suppressed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:26.040
and and people were sent to jail. Over a thousand&nbsp;
people were sent to jail. Many lives were ruined.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:33.000
And it was against that background&nbsp;
that the ACLU was formed in 1920 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:40.680
and began to fight. And, of course, you know, in&nbsp;
subsequent wars, we would see similar efforts at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:46.920
suppression, certainly during the Vietnam War.
Speaker 1: But what modern administration and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:51.780
we can include the Vietnam War era in that&nbsp;
I mean, you know, in the last 80 or so years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:57.540
was most harmful, I guess is the word I would&nbsp;
use when it comes to suppression of speech. Is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:03.000
there is there a clear administration, I guess.
Speaker 5: Well, yeah, Wilson clearly stands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:08.700
out because there was so there was so little&nbsp;
law actually on the First Amendment before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:13.920
the 20th century. And there had been a whole&nbsp;
century before and go, you know, go all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:19.800
way back to the Alien and Sedition Acts right&nbsp;
at the turn of the 18th century into the 19th.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:27.360
Newspaper editors were thrown into jail for&nbsp;
criticizing the administration of John Adams.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:38.040
And so so there was but there was very&nbsp;
little law on First Amendment rights before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:45.420
you know, before World War One. So and then,&nbsp;
you know, the there were certainly efforts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:51.120
during the the the Nixon administration around&nbsp;
the Pentagon Papers, efforts to suppress. Right.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:59.280
You know, that very critical study of the Vietnam&nbsp;
War. You know, Daniel Ellsberg was actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:06.600
indicted for sharing that with The New York Times&nbsp;
and The Washington Post and and might well have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:13.789
gone to jail if the Supreme Court hadn't stepped&nbsp;
in and said that that that was protected speech.&nbsp;

00:04:13.789 --> 00:04:18.540
Speaker 1: Are you surprised or I guess what&nbsp;
do you make of how the American right wing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:23.580
recently has very proactively claimed&nbsp;
free speech as one of its core tenets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:29.880
despite sort of not only having a shaky history on&nbsp;
the subject in the past, but even while claiming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:36.900
that as one of its tenets, you know, talking about&nbsp;
Trump wants to jail flag burners or open up libel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:42.600
laws, which basically is code for make it easier&nbsp;
to sue newspapers and media outlets for doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:51.780
journalism. Is it is it surprising historically&nbsp;
that you would see that seeming conflict? And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:57.180
what else do you sort of think about that?
Speaker 5: Well, it's wildly ironic. Yeah, it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.180 --> 00:05:05.940
wildly ironic to hear conservatives advocating&nbsp;
as defenders of free speech when over the course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:12.840
of American history they have been the major&nbsp;
violators of, uh, of, of that freedom, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:19.440
they've often been in the majority. And the people&nbsp;
who have been advocating for free speech have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:24.600
people fighting for equal rights. And that was&nbsp;
something that, you know, they opposed. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:33.780
you know, this goes back to that goes back to the&nbsp;
abolitionists and to the women, women's rights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:42.120
activists and activists for sexual freedom. You&nbsp;
know, they you know, they have at every turn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:47.760
conservatives have tried to suppress suppress&nbsp;
their speech and right through the civil rights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:53.100
movement, of course, and into the into the modern&nbsp;
era. And as you say, Donald Trump was a big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.120 --> 00:06:00.540
and, you know, waved the big stick against the&nbsp;
press, called the press the enemy of the people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:09.720
So, yes, to suddenly see conservatives&nbsp;
advocating, you know, for free speech rights is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:15.240
ironic and also difficult for us because it&nbsp;
alienates our kind of our traditional base,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:19.260
which is young people and&nbsp;
liberals and progressives who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:25.560
have been part of those equal rights fights.
Speaker 1: There are many accusations hurled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:32.880
at the left about suppressing speech, including&nbsp;
different types of alleged censorship on college&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:40.140
campuses, both when it comes to alleged lack of&nbsp;
political diversity among faculty, but also as it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:47.700
pertains to invited speakers to college campuses,&nbsp;
has become a very big kind of flashpoint to some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:54.600
degree along the lines of LGBT discussions. Most&nbsp;
recently, when it comes to trans rights, the right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.600 --> 00:07:02.280
will sometimes hurl allegations at the left. What&nbsp;
do you think is the biggest area of concern on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:09.540
the left when it comes to stifling free speech?
Speaker 5: Well, I think you know, I think and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:16.860
that, again, is, you know, a change in the&nbsp;
political climate around free speech. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:25.980
you know, I think that it's understandable in a&nbsp;
world that's suffused now with misinformation and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.980 --> 00:07:35.100
hate speech, that that people progressives have&nbsp;
sometimes tried to suppress speech themselves. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:41.640
we are concerned about those efforts on campus.&nbsp;
And we are concerned about we certainly support&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:48.840
the right to protest speakers and on campus and&nbsp;
to and to present the other view. But we really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:54.540
think it's it's a mistake to try to suppress&nbsp;
them, because then conservatives just turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.540 --> 00:07:59.389
around and point to that as justification&nbsp;
for the suppression that they want it.&nbsp;

00:07:59.389 --> 00:08:02.220
Speaker 1: Right. Yeah. My view&nbsp;
on this has been, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:07.800
an unsavory guest is invited to a campus,&nbsp;
someone I'm not interested in seeing. Fine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:17.160
If they have been properly invited and there's&nbsp;
no criminal issue and whatever else certainly in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:23.160
there's nothing wrong with the invitation and the&nbsp;
fact that they have an event protesting outside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:30.000
the event. Absolutely fine with it. The protesters&nbsp;
shouldn't be restricted to some pen a block and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:36.600
half away where nobody sees that you're absolutely&nbsp;
fine. Once the event starts, it does seem to me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:43.020
that it's correct to allow the speaker to&nbsp;
speak, and then you can continue expressing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.020 --> 00:08:48.300
your displeasure during a Q&amp;A, for example, the&nbsp;
counterpoint will be, Well, there might be no Q&amp;A,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.300 --> 00:08:55.680
so your only opportunity is while the Speaker is&nbsp;
speaking, or may I understand that it starts to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.680 --> 00:09:01.380
get to one of these things of, well, you know,&nbsp;
the protests can't only be the way everybody's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:06.300
comfortable. Part of protesting historically is&nbsp;
sometimes the protests happen in a way and at a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:11.760
time and in a place where it is an imposition on&nbsp;
someone else. But fundamentally, I do think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:17.820
preventing the speaker from speaking is a mistake.
Speaker 5: Yeah. And I think it also applies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.820 --> 00:09:25.020
to trying to revoke invitations that have been&nbsp;
made to speakers. If there are people on campus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.020 --> 00:09:30.720
who want to hear that speaker, they should&nbsp;
have the right to hear them. But by the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:39.240
token, that those who don't have an absolute right&nbsp;
to protest and but we don't want to you know, we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:45.540
don't want to say free speech only for our side,&nbsp;
which is really what the conservatives are saying.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:53.040
It's it is free speech for us and not for you,&nbsp;
because then that lays the groundwork for what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:59.640
we are seeing now around the country, which is&nbsp;
this hundreds and hundreds of challenges to books&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:04.980
in schools and libraries that progressives care&nbsp;
deeply about. These are books about race and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:13.200
persistence of racism in America and how to look&nbsp;
at slavery in and around the lives of LGBT people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:21.180
and, you know, their right to be represented&nbsp;
in school libraries. And and so, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.180 --> 00:10:26.880
we can't we have to protect this is the classic&nbsp;
statement. We have to protect the rights of all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:32.520
in order to ensure that everybody has rights.
Speaker 1: When it comes to the book, banning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:41.280
you know, the truth, as I have researched it,&nbsp;
is that the right is overwhelmingly the side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:48.240
that has the lists of books that should&nbsp;
not be in libraries. But very often the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:54.240
right will hurl back at the left, look at what&nbsp;
happened with Dr. Seuss. They cancel Dr. Seuss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.240 --> 00:11:01.020
etc. To me, it does not seem analogous for&nbsp;
a publisher to decide we're not going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.020 --> 00:11:07.140
continue new editions of certain books because&nbsp;
some of the imagery or messaging is outdated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.140 --> 00:11:12.840
based on current norms. That's very different&nbsp;
than Here's a list of 20 books that shouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.840 --> 00:11:17.580
even be available in the library. From your&nbsp;
perspective, are these two very different things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:22.200
or are they equivalent the way the rights states?
Speaker 5: No, I don't think they're equivalent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:28.680
I think that in their in the cases are very&nbsp;
slim know the cases that they can point to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.680 --> 00:11:38.040
of alleged suppression of books certainly just&nbsp;
don't hold up to the overwhelming majority of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.040 --> 00:11:45.180
challenges to books in schools are coming from&nbsp;
coming from conservatives and parents who just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.180 --> 00:11:51.900
don't want their kids or anybody else's kids&nbsp;
to have access to books that in many cases are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.900 --> 00:11:58.500
lifesaving because they they show LGBT kids&nbsp;
that they belong, that they are accepted.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.940 --> 00:12:04.440
And these are you know, these are very important&nbsp;
books and also how we present our history.&nbsp;

00:12:04.440 --> 00:12:11.700
Speaker 1: Last thing I want to ask you about.&nbsp;
What do you make of the tie between actual issues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.700 --> 00:12:17.100
of suppression of speech by governments and some&nbsp;
of the sort of cancel culture, social media,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.100 --> 00:12:22.620
private business discussion that now is going&nbsp;
on? Certainly legally. I think at this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.620 --> 00:12:25.980
most of us, although some people call me&nbsp;
and they don't seem to get this difference,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.980 --> 00:12:31.740
most of us understand that legally Twitter can&nbsp;
have terms of service wherein they don't allow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.740 --> 00:12:37.080
certain types of statements to be made. It's&nbsp;
not a First Amendment issue. It has nothing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.080 --> 00:12:41.160
do with that that we understand. So we can&nbsp;
sort of skip beyond that. But ideologically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.940 --> 00:12:46.500
and from a principals standpoint,&nbsp;
when there is not a legal issue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.500 --> 00:12:52.680
what would an organization like yours&nbsp;
say about some of the terms of service&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.680 --> 00:12:56.460
on these social media platforms that&nbsp;
do limit the things that are allowed?&nbsp;

00:12:58.020 --> 00:13:06.300
Speaker 5: But we understand the power of&nbsp;
social media and and we understand why the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.300 --> 00:13:13.020
decisions that get made on social media are&nbsp;
so are so important to people. We have we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.020 --> 00:13:18.300
an arts advocacy project ourselves, you know,&nbsp;
trying to push back against algorithms that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.300 --> 00:13:24.720
banning all nudity, for example, including&nbsp;
artistic nudity from from social media.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.060 --> 00:13:32.760
But ultimately, you know, we hold with the view&nbsp;
that that the companies have to be allowed as as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.760 --> 00:13:39.420
is their First Amendment right to make decisions&nbsp;
about what content they allow and don't allow.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.420 --> 00:13:47.400
And, you know, and and unfortunately, at this&nbsp;
point, you know, there are efforts underway.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.400 --> 00:13:51.540
There are laws that have recently been passed&nbsp;
in Texas and in Florida that are trying to force&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.680 --> 00:13:58.200
social media companies to accept content that they&nbsp;
don't want. Right. And that, you know, that is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.200 --> 00:14:04.500
step toward government, you know, government&nbsp;
intervention in free speech that we just can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.500 --> 00:14:07.680
we can't allow that we have to challenge.
Speaker 1: Yeah, that's an important point.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:12.540
It's ultimately government intervention&nbsp;
either way. And that's that's a critical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.540 --> 00:14:16.680
point we've been speaking with Chris&nbsp;
final. And who's the executive director&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.680 --> 00:14:22.200
of the National Coalition Against Censorship?&nbsp;
The book is How Free Speech Saved Democracy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.200 --> 00:14:26.280
which we will link to from our YouTube&nbsp;
clip for this interview. Chris,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.280 --> 00:14:29.520
really appreciate your time today.
Speaker 5: Thanks for having me.

